<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <!--
    ros2_control hardware description for a standard yam arm + linear_4310
    gripper on a single CAN bus, driven by
    i2rt_hardware_interface/YamSystemInterface.

    motor_id/motor_type/kp/kd/gravity_comp_factor/compliant_kd values below
    are i2rt-robotics/i2rt's yam_v1.yml (i2rt/robots/config/yam_v1.yml) as of
    2026-09-22, and this arm's gripper is the same linear_4310 unit as
    big_yam's (i2rt/robots/config/linear_4310.yml: motor_type DM4310,
    motor_kp 20.0, motor_kd 0.5 - arm-agnostic). Unlike big_yam (DM6248 on
    joints1-2, DM4340 on joints3-4), this arm uses DM4340 on joints1-3 and
    DM4310 on joints4-6 only - confirm this against the physical motors
    before activating real hardware.

    direction has been hardware-verified on two real yam units (2026-09-22):
    joints 1-5 confirmed correct as yam_v1.yml's raw values (all 1), joint6
    confirmed needing a flip to -1 - see that joint's own comment. Offsets
    (all 0.0) have NOT been independently verified beyond direction - if a
    joint's commanded vs. real position disagrees by a fixed amount rather
    than a sign flip, check offset next.

    gravity_comp_factor/compliant_kd semantics match
    big_yam_ros2_control.xacro's macro comment exactly - see there for the
    full explanation of compliant_mode and the gravity feed-forward.
    gravity_comp_factor values below are NOT yam_v1.yml's raw
    ([1.0, 1.1, 1.1, 1.2, 1.0, 1.0]) - re-tuned/hardware-verified on real
    hardware instead, tip-to-base (joint5 to joint2), each joint's own
    comment has the full story:
      - joint1: untouched (1.0, yam_v1.yml's own value) - never showed a
        problem, no re-tuning attempted.
      - joint2: 0.9 (validated).
      - joint3: 0.9 (validated).
      - joint4: 0.95 (validated).
      - joint5: 0.0 PERMANENTLY, not a placeholder - see its comment for why
        (real hardware friction already holds it; active comp made things
        worse). Verified on two separate physical units.
      - joint6: untouched (1.0, yam_v1.yml's own value) - never showed a
        problem, no re-tuning attempted.
    Re-tuning was needed because yam_v1.yml's raw values, applied as-is,
    caused joints 2-5 to actively fight/oscillate in compliant_mode rather
    than just support their own weight - see the yam_ros2_control.xacro
    section of this repo's conversation/commit history around 2026-09-22 for
    the full diagnostic trail (symptoms, what was ruled out, the tip-to-base
    braced testing methodology used to get clean single-joint measurements).

    joint2/3/4 were re-tuned TWICE: an initial pass found joint4 apparently
    needed a per-unit value (0.5 vs 0.45 between two units), which looked
    like real unit-to-unit variance at the time. It wasn't - link_6 (the
    wrist-roll link) was wrongly carrying the linear_4310 gripper's full
    housing mesh and ~0.55 kg mass even in bare-wrist (use_gripper:=false)
    mode (see yam_macro.xacro's link_6 comment for the evidence trail: the
    upstream i2rt reference model has NO geometry there at all when no
    gripper is attached). Once that was fixed, both units re-tuned to
    IDENTICAL values (0.9/0.9/0.95) - including joint4's separate "pulls
    toward one hard stop near center" symptom on one unit, which turned out
    to be the same phantom-mass bug (a distal-mass modeling error shifting
    the predicted gravity zero-crossing) and is now confirmed gone. The
    per-unit override entries in i2rt_description/launch/arm_registry.py
    were removed once both units converged - see that file's git history if
    a genuine per-unit difference ever needs to be reintroduced there.
    gravity_comp_factor is also live-tunable per joint at runtime, without
    relaunching, via a ROS parameter on the hardware component's internal
    diagnostics node - see yam_system_interface.hpp's class comment
    (gravity compensation section) for the exact parameter name/usage; this
    is how the values above were actually found.

    yam_v1.yml also has a per-joint "coulomb_friction" array
    ([0.3, 0.3, 0.3, 0.06, 0.06, 0.06]) that YamSystemInterface does not
    currently consume at all (the upstream Python driver treats it as an
    optional, off-by-default feed-forward term) - not wired up here, kept
    only as a note in case gravity comp alone proves insufficient.

    max_gravity_torque_nm/gravity_root_link/gravity_tip_link: kept at the
    same default as big_yam_ros2_control.xacro (25.0 Nm, base_link/link_6) as
    a starting point - not independently derived for this arm's (lighter)
    mass distribution. Watch for "Gravity torque for joint '...' clamped"
    warnings in the log on real hardware and adjust if seen; see
    yam_system_interface.hpp's class comment.

    use_gripper:=false omits this macro's gripper_joint <ros2_control> block
    entirely (see yam_linear_4310.urdf.xacro, which also skips instantiating
    the gripper_linear_4310 URDF macro in that case) - use for an arm with no
    gripper physically installed. Defaults to true.

    gripper_joint (motor 7) calibration_lower_limit_m/calibration_upper_limit_m
    are copied from big_yam_ros2_control.xacro's values (same physical
    linear_4310 gripper hardware and gripper_linear_4310.xacro finger joint
    limits), and calibration_polarity is left at its default (1.0) since
    big_yam's -1.0 was empirically discovered on that specific unit's wiring,
    not derived from any config file - verify on this arm's actual gripper
    before trusting it (see run_gripper_calibrations(), and keep the gripper
    clear of obstructions the first time).

    use_mock_hardware:=true swaps in ros2_control's built-in
    mock_components/GenericSystem instead of the real CAN-backed plugin - see
    big_yam_ros2_control.xacro's macro comment for the full explanation
    (applies unchanged here).

    compliant_mode:=true mirrors MotorChainRobot's zero_gravity_mode - see
    big_yam_ros2_control.xacro's macro comment for the full explanation
    (applies unchanged here).
  -->
  <xacro:macro name="yam_ros2_control"
    params="prefix can_channel:=can0 use_mock_hardware:=false compliant_mode:=false use_gripper:=true">

    <ros2_control name="${prefix}yam_system" type="system">
      <hardware>
        <xacro:if value="${use_mock_hardware}">
          <plugin>mock_components/GenericSystem</plugin>
        </xacro:if>
        <xacro:unless value="${use_mock_hardware}">
          <plugin>i2rt_hardware_interface/YamSystemInterface</plugin>
          <param name="can_channel">${can_channel}</param>
          <param name="gravity_root_link">${prefix}base_link</param>
          <param name="gravity_tip_link">${prefix}link_6</param>
          <param name="max_gravity_torque_nm">25.0</param>
          <!-- xacro coerces a "true"/"false" arg into a Python bool, which
               would render as capitalized "True"/"False" text if
               interpolated directly - force it back to the lowercase
               literal YamSystemInterface's on_init actually compares
               against. -->
          <param name="compliant_mode">${'true' if compliant_mode else 'false'}</param>
        </xacro:unless>
      </hardware>

      <joint name="${prefix}joint1">
        <param name="motor_id">1</param>
        <param name="motor_type">DM4340</param>
        <param name="direction">1</param>
        <param name="offset">0.0</param>
        <param name="kp">80.0</param>
        <param name="kd">5.0</param>
        <param name="gravity_comp_factor">1.0</param>
        <param name="compliant_kd">0.1</param>
        <command_interface name="position"/>
        <command_interface name="velocity"/>
        <command_interface name="effort"/>
        <command_interface name="kp"/>
        <command_interface name="kd"/>
        <state_interface name="position"/>
        <state_interface name="velocity"/>
        <state_interface name="effort"/>
      </joint>

      <joint name="${prefix}joint2">
        <param name="motor_id">2</param>
        <param name="motor_type">DM4340</param>
        <param name="direction">1</param>
        <param name="offset">0.0</param>
        <param name="kp">80.0</param>
        <param name="kd">5.0</param>
        <!-- Re-validated on real hardware (2026-09-22) after fixing
             link_6's gripper-housing mass being wrongly present even in
             bare-wrist (use_gripper:=false) mode - see yam_macro.xacro's
             link_6 comment. That phantom ~0.55 kg had been the actual
             source of what looked like unit-to-unit variance requiring
             per-arm overrides (see i2rt_description/launch/arm_registry.py
             history - yam1/yam2 needed different values before this fix,
             identical values after); promoted back to this single shared
             default once both units converged. Originally tuned via
             tip-to-base re-tuning (braced upstream joints, live-tunable
             gravity_comp_factor.joint2 parameter on diagnostics_node_ - see
             this file's/the .hpp's class comment) - not yam_v1.yml's
             original 1.1, never independently verified. -->
        <param name="gravity_comp_factor">0.9</param>
        <param name="compliant_kd">0.1</param>
        <command_interface name="position"/>
        <command_interface name="velocity"/>
        <command_interface name="effort"/>
        <command_interface name="kp"/>
        <command_interface name="kd"/>
        <state_interface name="position"/>
        <state_interface name="velocity"/>
        <state_interface name="effort"/>
      </joint>

      <joint name="${prefix}joint3">
        <param name="motor_id">3</param>
        <param name="motor_type">DM4340</param>
        <param name="direction">1</param>
        <param name="offset">0.0</param>
        <param name="kp">80.0</param>
        <param name="kd">5.0</param>
        <!-- Re-validated on real hardware (2026-09-22) after fixing
             link_6's gripper-housing mass being wrongly present even in
             bare-wrist (use_gripper:=false) mode - see yam_macro.xacro's
             link_6 comment and joint2's comment above for the full story
             (the phantom mass was the real source of apparent unit-to-unit
             variance; promoted back to a single shared default once yam1/
             yam2 converged on the same value post-fix). Originally tuned
             via tip-to-base re-tuning (braced upstream joints) - not
             yam_v1.yml's original 1.1, never independently verified; an
             earlier attempt starting from it (1.1->2.2->-1.1, before the
             upstream-drift confound was understood) produced confusing,
             unreliable results - superseded by the braced re-test. -->
        <param name="gravity_comp_factor">0.9</param>
        <param name="compliant_kd">0.1</param>
        <command_interface name="position"/>
        <command_interface name="velocity"/>
        <command_interface name="effort"/>
        <command_interface name="kp"/>
        <command_interface name="kd"/>
        <state_interface name="position"/>
        <state_interface name="velocity"/>
        <state_interface name="effort"/>
      </joint>

      <joint name="${prefix}joint4">
        <param name="motor_id">4</param>
        <param name="motor_type">DM4310</param>
        <param name="direction">1</param>
        <param name="offset">0.0</param>
        <param name="kp">10.0</param>
        <param name="kd">1.5</param>
        <!-- Re-validated on real hardware (2026-09-22) after fixing
             link_6's gripper-housing mass being wrongly present even in
             bare-wrist (use_gripper:=false) mode - see yam_macro.xacro's
             link_6 comment. This joint saw the LARGEST swing of the three
             (0.45 -> 0.95): before the fix, this joint also exhibited a
             real "pulls toward one hard stop near the middle of its range"
             symptom on one unit (yam2) - consistent with the phantom distal
             mass shifting the predicted gravity zero-crossing away from the
             real one. Confirmed GONE after this fix, at this value, on that
             same unit - not a compromise anymore, holds well through the
             full range. Previously required a per-unit override (0.5 vs
             0.45) that turned out to be the bug, not real hardware
             variance - both units converged on 0.95 after the fix; see
             i2rt_description/launch/arm_registry.py's history. Not
             yam_v1.yml's original 1.2, never independently verified. -->
        <param name="gravity_comp_factor">0.95</param>
        <param name="compliant_kd">0.3</param>
        <command_interface name="position"/>
        <command_interface name="velocity"/>
        <command_interface name="effort"/>
        <command_interface name="kp"/>
        <command_interface name="kd"/>
        <state_interface name="position"/>
        <state_interface name="velocity"/>
        <state_interface name="effort"/>
      </joint>

      <joint name="${prefix}joint5">
        <param name="motor_id">5</param>
        <param name="motor_type">DM4310</param>
        <param name="direction">1</param>
        <param name="offset">0.0</param>
        <param name="kp">10.0</param>
        <param name="kd">1.5</param>
        <!-- Gravity comp intentionally left at 0.0 PERMANENTLY (2026-09-22),
             not a pending/temporary value like joints 2/3/4 below. Confirmed
             on real hardware: with the motor fully unpowered (no control
             loop of any kind, not even kd damping), this joint holds its
             position well on its own - real mechanical friction (gearbox/
             cable routing) not modeled anywhere in this codebase (no
             Coulomb-friction term is implemented; see yam_v1.yml's unused
             coulomb_friction field) is doing that work passively. Combined
             with light real distal weight (no end effector currently
             installed), active gravity comp here was doing more harm than
             good: a small, imperfect feed-forward torque interacting with
             that real, unmodeled friction produced confusing, sharply
             nonlinear behavior when testing (mild resistance at 0.3, a full
             runaway at 0.6 - friction stick-slip/breakaway effects, not a
             linear response to a simple scalar). Since the joint already
             does its job passively, don't re-enable this without new
             evidence it's actually needed (e.g. a real end effector
             installed that changes the weight/friction balance). -->
        <param name="gravity_comp_factor">0.0</param>
        <param name="compliant_kd">0.05</param>
        <command_interface name="position"/>
        <command_interface name="velocity"/>
        <command_interface name="effort"/>
        <command_interface name="kp"/>
        <command_interface name="kd"/>
        <state_interface name="position"/>
        <state_interface name="velocity"/>
        <state_interface name="effort"/>
      </joint>

      <joint name="${prefix}joint6">
        <param name="motor_id">6</param>
        <param name="motor_type">DM4310</param>
        <!-- Hardware-verified on two real yam units (2026-09-22): commanding
             a rotation direction moved the joint opposite to it in RViz with
             yam_v1.yml's raw direction of 1 - flipped to -1 to correct, same
             kind of hardware-specific sign flip big_yam's joint4/joint6
             needed relative to their own .yml (see this file's header
             comment). -->
        <param name="direction">-1</param>
        <param name="offset">0.0</param>
        <param name="kp">10.0</param>
        <param name="kd">1.5</param>
        <param name="gravity_comp_factor">1.0</param>
        <param name="compliant_kd">0.05</param>
        <command_interface name="position"/>
        <command_interface name="velocity"/>
        <command_interface name="effort"/>
        <command_interface name="kp"/>
        <command_interface name="kd"/>
        <state_interface name="position"/>
        <state_interface name="velocity"/>
        <state_interface name="effort"/>
      </joint>

      <xacro:if value="${use_gripper}">
        <joint name="${prefix}gripper_joint">
          <param name="motor_id">7</param>
          <param name="motor_type">DM4310</param>
          <param name="kp">20.0</param>
          <param name="kd">0.5</param>
          <param name="direction">1.0</param>
          <param name="offset">0.0</param>
          <param name="gravity_comp_factor">0.0</param>
          <param name="requires_calibration">true</param>
          <!-- Not empirically verified on this arm's specific gripper unit
               yet - big_yam's own -1.0 was only discovered by testing real
               hardware, not derived from any config. Check open/close
               behavior against real hardware and correct if backwards. -->
          <param name="calibration_polarity">1.0</param>
          <param name="calibration_lower_limit_m">-0.0475</param>
          <param name="calibration_upper_limit_m">0.0</param>
          <command_interface name="position"/>
          <command_interface name="velocity"/>
          <command_interface name="effort"/>
          <command_interface name="kp"/>
          <command_interface name="kd"/>
          <state_interface name="position"/>
          <state_interface name="velocity"/>
          <state_interface name="effort"/>
        </joint>
      </xacro:if>

    </ros2_control>

  </xacro:macro>

</robot>
